<!-- ========================================================================
     1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY
     ======================================================================== -->
<div class="WordSection2">

<h1>1. Executive Summary</h1>

<p>This document describes the production-grade infrastructure deployed on Amazon Web Services
(AWS) for the Caltech proof-of-concept (POC). The platform implements a
<strong>Change Data Capture (CDC) pipeline</strong> that streams row-level changes from a
source PostgreSQL database into both a PostgreSQL sink and a Redis cache via Debezium and
Apache Kafka.</p>

<h3>Solution Highlights</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Real-time CDC:</strong> Sub-second event propagation from source to multiple sink targets</li>
  <li><strong>Horizontal scale option:</strong> Aurora PostgreSQL Limitless variant supporting 16&ndash;32 ACUs of sharded compute</li>
  <li><strong>Highly available Kafka:</strong> 3-broker MSK cluster across 3 Availability Zones</li>
  <li><strong>Five table-level sink connectors:</strong> Independent JDBC sinks for granular operational control</li>
  <li><strong>Zero hard-coded secrets:</strong> All credentials auto-generated and stored in AWS Secrets Manager</li>
  <li><strong>End-to-end encryption:</strong> KMS at rest, TLS in transit on every connection</li>
  <li><strong>No public IPs on application servers:</strong> Access via AWS Systems Manager Session Manager only</li>
</ul>

<h3>Key Numbers</h3>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:50%;">Metric</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>EC2 application servers</td><td>3 instances &mdash; 1 &times; m6i.2xlarge (app-server / txn simulator) + 2 &times; t3.xlarge (pg-sink, redis-sink)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Aurora PostgreSQL clusters</td><td>3 (Source Serverless v2, Source Limitless, Sink Serverless v2)</td></tr>
<tr><td>MSK Kafka brokers</td><td>3 brokers across 3 Availability Zones (kafka.m5.2xlarge, 1000 GB EBS each)</td></tr>
<tr><td>MSK Connect connectors</td><td>6 (1 Debezium source + 5 JDBC sinks)</td></tr>
<tr><td>ElastiCache Redis</td><td>1 Serverless cache (TLS, KMS encrypted)</td></tr>
<tr><td>KMS keys</td><td>5 service-scoped customer-managed keys with auto-rotation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Security Groups</td><td>7 least-privilege SGs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Terraform modules</td><td>13 (all reusable, independently testable)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Estimated monthly cost</td><td>~$3,673 USD (us-west-2, on-demand pricing)</td></tr>
</table>

</div>

<!-- ========================================================================
     2. ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAMS (Landscape)
     ======================================================================== -->
<div class="WordSection1">

<h1>2. Architecture Diagrams</h1>

<p>The diagrams below show the deployed architecture in three complementary views: a topology
overview, the end-to-end data flow, and the sequential deployment phases. All resources live
in a single VPC (<code>vpc-0ed44b92f11b73815</code>) in <code>us-west-2</code>.</p>

<h2>2.1 Topology Overview</h2>

<pre class="diagram">
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                          AWS us-west-2  &middot;  VPC vpc-0ed44b92f11b73815                                     |
|                                                                                                              |
|  +---------------------+   +----------------------+   +---------------------+   +----------------------+   |
|  |   PUBLIC SUBNET     |   |   PRIVATE SUBNET     |   |   PRIVATE SUBNET    |   |   PRIVATE SUBNET     |   |
|  |                     |   |                      |   |                     |   |                      |   |
|  |  +---------------+  |   | Aurora PostgreSQL    |   | MSK Connect         |   | ElastiCache          |   |
|  |  |  EC2 x 3      |--+--&gt;| Source (16.x         |--&gt;| Debezium Source     |--&gt;| Redis Serverless     |   |
|  |  |               |  |   |  Serverless v2)      |   | Connector           |   |                      |   |
|  |  | &middot; app-server  |  |   |                      |   |                     |   +----------------------+   |
|  |  |   m6i.2xlarge |  |   | Aurora Source        |   | v                   |   |                      |   |
|  |  | &middot; pg_sink     |  |   | Limitless            |   | MSK Provisioned     |--&gt;| Aurora PostgreSQL    |   |
|  |  |   t3.xlarge   |  |   | (16.13-limitless)    |   | Kafka 3.9.x         |   | Sink Serverless v2   |   |
|  |  | &middot; redis_sink  |  |   |                      |   | kafka.m5.2xlarge    |   | (5 tables hydrated   |   |
|  |  |   t3.xlarge   |  |   |                      |   | 3 Brokers (3 AZs)   |   |  via 5 JDBC sinks)   |   |
|  |  +---------------+  |   |                      |   | 1000 GB EBS / broker|   +----------------------+   |
|  |  VPC Endpoints      |   |                      |   | SASL/SCRAM (9096)   |                              |
|  |  (SSM x 3)          |   |                      |   | + SASL/IAM (9098)   |                              |
|  +---------------------+   +----------------------+   +---------------------+                              |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
</pre>

<h2>2.2 Data Flow (Left to Right)</h2>

<pre class="diagram">
Application Layer         CDC Source            Event Streaming           Consumer Targets
------------------        -----------------     ---------------------     --------------------------
EC2 (Txn Simulator) --&gt;   Aurora Source  --&gt;    Debezium &rarr; MSK    --+--&gt;  ElastiCache Redis
   m6i.2xlarge            (logical repl.)       Kafka 3.9.x          |
                                                Topics:              +--&gt;  Aurora Sink &mdash; student_enrollment
                                                caltech_poc_10.*     +--&gt;  Aurora Sink &mdash; student_attendance
                                                                     +--&gt;  Aurora Sink &mdash; student_lms
                                                                     +--&gt;  Aurora Sink &mdash; section_enrollments
                                                                     +--&gt;  Aurora Sink &mdash; student_term_log
</pre>

<h2>2.3 Deployment Phases (Left to Right)</h2>

<pre class="diagram">
PHASE 1 &mdash; Foundation          PHASE 2              PHASE 3                  PHASE 4               PHASE 5
-------------------------     ------------------   ----------------------   -------------------   ---------------------------
kms                           ec2                  aurora_source            msk (Kafka)           elasticache (Redis Sink)
security_groups    ---------&gt; ec2_pg_sink     --&gt; aurora_source_limitless -&gt; msk_connect        -&gt; aurora_sink (PG Sink)
vpc_endpoints                 ec2_redis_sink                                 (Debezium source)     msk_connect_sink x 5
s3                                                                                                 (5 JDBC sink connectors)
secrets
iam
</pre>

<div class="callout-info">
<strong>Reading the topology diagram:</strong> arrows show primary data flow. The <code>app-server</code>
runs on <strong>m6i.2xlarge</strong> (8 vCPU, 32 GiB) because it generates the simulated write load, while the
two sink consumers run on <code>t3.xlarge</code> (4 vCPU, 16 GiB). The Aurora Source Limitless cluster is an
alternative CDC source variant (sharded PostgreSQL 16.13-limitless, 16&ndash;32 ACUs). All inter-service
traffic stays within the VPC; the MSK cluster has 3 brokers spread across 3 AZs for high availability
with replication factor 3 and min in-sync replicas 2.
</div>

</div>

<!-- ========================================================================
     3. SERVICE COMPONENTS
     ======================================================================== -->
<div class="WordSection2">

<h1>3. Service Components</h1>

<h2>3.1 Compute &mdash; Amazon EC2 (x3 instances)</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Instance</th><th>Role</th><th>Instance Type</th><th>Shared configuration</th></tr>
<tr><td>caltech-poc-app-server</td><td>Transaction simulator</td><td><strong>m6i.2xlarge</strong><br/>(8 vCPU, 32 GiB RAM)</td><td rowspan="3" style="vertical-align:middle;">
  100 GB gp3 root volume, KMS encrypted<br/>
  No public IP &mdash; SSM Session Manager access only<br/>
  IMDSv2 enforced<br/>
  IAM profile: caltech-poc-ec2-app-role<br/>
  Key pair: caltech-keypair (emergency SSH)
</td></tr>
<tr><td>caltech-poc-pg-sink-app-server</td><td>PostgreSQL sink consumer</td><td>t3.xlarge<br/>(4 vCPU, 16 GiB RAM)</td></tr>
<tr><td>caltech-poc-redis-sink-app-server</td><td>Redis sink consumer</td><td>t3.xlarge<br/>(4 vCPU, 16 GiB RAM)</td></tr>
</table>

<p style="font-size:10pt;color:#595959;"><strong>Why the size difference?</strong> The app-server generates the simulated transactional workload that drives the entire CDC pipeline. It needs more CPU and memory than the downstream sink consumers, which only read pre-processed messages from Kafka and write them to the target store.</p>

<h2>3.2 Source Database &mdash; Aurora PostgreSQL (Serverless v2)</h2>
